Yes. The deeper object is not “a face” at all. It is a point or distribution in a learned representation space, produced by a function.

Very roughly:

image x -> encoder f(x) -> latent vector z

Then identity/similarity is no longer handled only as pixels. It becomes geometry: distance, direction, clustering, neighborhood structure, invariants, symmetries, and trajectories in that latent space.

That is where your Pantheon move becomes powerful, because once something is represented mathematically, you can operate on the representation rather than the surface:

z -> transform T(z) -> z'

Then ask what survives, what changes, and what the transform means.

The most useful pieces are things like invariants and equivariances. An invariant is something that remains stable under a transformation. An equivariant feature changes predictably when the input changes. Those are gold because they tell you which parts of a pattern are essential versus incidental.

Then you can lift the operator out:

face representation -> geometry of identity

and ask whether the same kind of geometry exists in:

business -> latent organizational state
language -> semantic embeddings
biology -> phenotype/genotype spaces
markets -> regime/state spaces
software -> execution/state representations

That is the real “surfing” move: not copying a face model into business, but extracting the deeper operator:

encode -> find geometry -> transform -> predict -> decode/test

Your knight’s-move idea fits too, as a research heuristic. Instead of moving directly from domain A to B, move across two axes:

biology -> geometry -> software
story -> control theory -> business
morphology -> topology -> law

That can expose analogies that a direct transfer misses.

The caveat is that latent spaces are not magic truth spaces. They reflect the model, the data, the training objective, and the metric chosen. So every latent operator needs a validation loop:

representation -> hypothesis -> predicted effect -> observed effect -> delta

I added this as Latent Geometry / Representation Surfing / Functional Transduction.

That one is central, because it gives Pantheon a place to live that is deeper than any one medium: the space of transformations between representations.